  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through Sunday)\nIssued at 205 AM CST Mon Nov 9 2026\n\nBy Thursday into Friday, a longwave trough is forecast to develop\nacross the eastern half of the country. As a result, a stronger, \nreinforcing cold front will advance southward as the trough \namplifies to the east. This will bring cooler weather for Thursday\nand Friday with temperatures likely closer to or even slightly \nbelow average (mid 50s to mid 60s deg F). \n\nThe cooldown will likely be brief with northwest flow aloft. This\nis because when the Southern Plains is in northwest flow, we are \nusually on the edge of downslope warming off the high terrain to \nthe north and west. So after a cooldown, temperatures can quickly \nrise as that downslope-warmed air mass moves into the area from \nthe northwest. As a result, warmer weather is expected by the \nweekend.\n\nLow rain chances return by Sunday into Monday as a trough is \nforecast to approach the Plains from the southwest. However, there\nis wide variation among deterministic models and their ensemble \nmembers on the exact track of the wave (e.g., some members have a \nsoutherly track across South Texas). Therefore, the rain chances \nare only ~20 to 30% at this time.\n\nMahale",
